{"schema":"clawhub.skill.verify.v1","ok":false,"decision":"fail","reasons":["security.status_not_clean"],"slug":"nexo-brain","displayName":"NEXO Desktop Runtime","pageUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/wazionapps/skills/nexo-brain","publisherHandle":"wazionapps","publisherDisplayName":"wazionapps","publisherProfileUrl":"https://clawhub.ai/wazionapps","version":"7.38.7","resolvedFrom":"version","tag":null,"createdAt":1782628196486,"card":{"available":true,"path":"skill-card.md","url":"https://wry-manatee-359.convex.site/api/v1/skills/nexo-brain/card?ownerHandle=wazionapps&version=7.38.7","sha256":"8ad8eadc91be0fe444b8d5ac7225e2058f786db55903faf60f899c1ce9d22697","size":1922,"contentType":"text/markdown; charset=utf-8"},"artifact":{"sourceFingerprint":"44b1d69eeec3c961389976f19b0cd9676e8524afbb361457b57711f39f57a718","bundleFingerprints":["e984cf7f27f0bab9ce5f558980d7d9f4449273aeb848319710dc7ac080a0a9c8","eb0c0dd46e988b2bd29b636d9eccad362a7fcb41d398efa52a99e14083c29717"],"files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":3453,"sha256":"159b907290f6896738d2bc073d1beffccc25249d676ef6688028a7b819363635","contentType":"text/markdown"}]},"provenance":{"source":"unavailable","reason":"No server-resolved GitHub import provenance is stored for this version."},"security":{"status":"suspicious","passed":false,"rawStatus":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","confidence":"medium","summary":"This is a coherent NEXO Desktop runtime installer, but it asks users to execute an unpinned npm package that can install a persistent local MCP runtime.","model":null,"checkedAt":1789432155815,"scannerReports":{"aig":{"version":"2.1.0","$schema":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/oasis-tcs/sarif-spec/main/Schemata/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json","runs":[{"tool":{"driver":{"name":"aig-skill-scan","version":"0.2.1","informationUri":"https://github.com/Tencent/AI-Infra-Guard","rules":[{"id":"T01","name":"Skill Instruction Hijacking","shortDescription":{"text":"Alters the agent's current session goals or safety constraints when the skill is loaded"}},{"id":"T02","name":"Agent Memory Poisoning","shortDescription":{"text":"Writes attacker-controlled rules into persistent memory so they continue to affect future sessions"}},{"id":"T03","name":"Remote Payload Retrieval and Execution","shortDescription":{"text":"Fetches code from an external URL, allowing the effective payload to change after skill review"}},{"id":"T04","name":"Embedded Malicious Code","shortDescription":{"text":"Ships malicious scripts inside the skill package and executes them locally when invoked"}},{"id":"T05","name":"Unauthorized Access and Privilege Escalation","shortDescription":{"text":"Breaks least-privilege boundaries by obtaining permissions beyond the task's legitimate needs"}},{"id":"T06","name":"System Persistence","shortDescription":{"text":"Installs cross-session backdoors, hooks, services, or scheduled tasks that survive the skill run"}},{"id":"T07","name":"Tool Hijacking and Spoofing","shortDescription":{"text":"Modifies, wraps, spoofs, or replaces tools so legitimate-looking calls execute attacker logic"}},{"id":"T08","name":"Insecure Dependencies","shortDescription":{"text":"Introduces malicious packages or components through dependency confusion, typosquatting, or unsafe sources"}},{"id":"T09","name":"Insecure Skill Coding Practices","shortDescription":{"text":"Exposes exploitable flaws such as hardcoded secrets, command injection, plaintext sensitive data, or unsafe temp files"}},{"id":"other","name":"Other Risk","shortDescription":{"text":"Security issues not covered by the T01-T09 classification"}}]}},"results":[{"ruleId":"T08","level":"error","message":{"text":"Unpinned Third-Party Package Is Downloaded and Executed"},"locations":[{"physicalLocation":{"artifactLocation":{"uri":"SKILL.md"},"region":{"startLine":15,"endLine":37}}}],"partialFingerprints":{"primaryLocationLineHash":"d11bcf6509aebc5c"},"properties":{"description":"<![CDATA[\n  ## Vulnerability Details\n  **File Location**: `SKILL.md:15-22` and `SKILL.md:33-37`  \n  **Vulnerability Type**: Unpinned executable dependency  \n  **Risk Level**: High\n\n  ### Vulnerable Code\n\n  ```yaml\n      install:\n        - id: npm\n          kind: node\n          package: nexo-brain\n          bins:\n            - nexo\n            - nexo-brain\n          label: Install NEXO Desktop runtime core (npm)\n  ```\n\n  ```markdown\n  If you are setting it up manually, install the runtime core:\n\n  ```bash\n  npx nexo-brain\n  ```\n  ```\n\n  ### Technical Analysis\n\n  The Skill instructs users to download and execute the `nexo-brain` npm package without specifying an audited version or package integrity hash. A bare `npx nexo-brain` invocation can resolve and execute the version currently published under that package name. Consequently, the code executed at installation time may change after the Skill itself has been reviewed.\n\n  The external package implementation is not included in the audited project. Its lifecycle scripts, executable entry points, generated files, and runtime behavior therefore cannot be verified from `SKILL.md`. This is a supply-chain trust boundary because npm package installation and execution can run code with the privileges of the invoking user.\n\n  The instructions subsequently configure `~/.nexo/server.py` as an MCP server. If the package installation creates or modifies that file, a compromised package could place attacker-controlled code at a location that OpenClaw is instructed to execute.\n\n  ### Attack Path\n\n  1. An attacker compromises the `nexo-brain` npm publisher account, publishing pipeline, or a future package release.\n  2. The attacker publishes a malicious version under the existing package name.\n  3. A user follows the Skill instructions and runs `npx nexo-brain` without a pinned version.\n  4. npm resolves the attacker-controlled release and executes its installation or executable code with the user's privileges.\n  5. The malicious package accesses user-readable data, modifies files, or writes an attacker-controlled `~/.nexo/server.py`.\n  6. The user adds that script to the OpenClaw MCP configuration as instructed.\n  7. Restarting or subsequently using the gateway executes the attacker-controlled MCP server in future sessions.\n\n  ### Impact Assessment\n\n  Successful exploitation permits arbitrary code execution under the account that invokes `npx`. The resulting access can include reading and modifying files available to that account, accessing local application state, altering OpenClaw configuration, and tampering with data stored under `~/.nexo/`.\n\n  If an attacker replaces the configured MCP server, malicious behavior may continue across later gateway sessions. The package does not inherently obtain administrator privileges from the reviewed instructions, so its direct access is limited to the invoking user's permissions unless additional local privilege-escalation vulnerabilities exist.\n  ]]>","severity":"High"},"fixes":[{"description":{"text":"<![CDATA[\n  ## Remediation Suggestions\n\n  1. Pin the dependency to a specifically reviewed version, for example `npx nexo-brain@<audited-version>`, rather than resolving the latest release.\n  2. Record and verify the package's registry integrity digest before execution. Use a lockfile or equivalent reproducible dependency manifest where possible.\n  3. Prefer installing from a controlled artifact repository or verified release archive with published checksums and signature validation.\n  4. Disable or separately inspect npm lifecycle scripts during installation when they are not required.\n  5. Document every file the installer creates, particularly `~/.nexo/server.py`, and verify its cryptographic hash against the audited release before registering it as an MCP server.\n  6. Run installation and the MCP server with the minimum required privileges, using filesystem and network isolation where practical.\n  7. Establish an update-review process so new package versions are not automatically trusted merely because the Skill documentation remains unchanged.\n  ]]>"}}]}],"properties":{"securityScore":60,"primaryLanguage":"Markdown","llm":"gpt-5.6","startTime":1789432072.801742,"endTime":1789432112.9880326}}]},"skillspector":{"skill":{"name":"nexo-brain","source":"/tmp/clawhub-codex-scan-v576vcw1jxkn6f6rqk7dcr89nh8edpf4-wxdgJc/clawscan-skillspector-1978763685/artifact","scanned_at":"2026-09-15T00:27:51.584030+00:00"},"risk_assessment":{"score":9,"severity":"LOW","recommendation":"SAFE","max_issue_severity":"MEDIUM"},"components":[{"path":"SKILL.md","type":"markdown","lines":101,"executable":false,"size_bytes":3453,"source_url":null,"source_identity":null,"source_digest":null}],"structured_summaries":[],"issues":[{"id":"RP1","finding_id":"finding-04eed25ee09840faa27dec5894ae44e5","category":"MCP Rug Pull","pattern":null,"severity":"MEDIUM","confidence":0.94,"location":{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":36,"end_line":null},"finding":null,"explanation":"The setup instructions tell users to run `npx nexo-brain` without pinning a specific version, which can fetch and execute whatever version is current at install time. Because this skill is a runtime core with memory, watchdog, and MCP/server capabilities, a compromised or malicious package update could gain broad local code-execution and persistence-related access on the user's machine.","remediation":"Pin the package to an exact version in the documentation, for example `npx nexo-brain@7.38.7`, and preferably provide integrity-checked or lockfile-based installation guidance. Also document verification steps such as validating the package source/publisher and avoiding implicit latest-version execution for security-sensitive runtime components.","code_snippet":null,"intent":null,"tags":["ASI16"],"evidence":{},"match_fingerprint":"da9e497f085b1adbc1f52311d27a581f2c4285dbaa9d32a4772008cfe608feba","occurrences":[{"file":"SKILL.md","start_line":36,"end_line":null}]}],"suppressed_count":0,"suppressed":[],